<h2>The statutory route, briefly</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Reference.</strong> The supplier (or either party) refers the delayed-payment dispute to the MSEFC under Section 18(1).</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Conciliation.</strong> The Council conducts or refers conciliation, applying the conciliation provisions of the 1996 Act.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Arbitration.</strong> On failure, Section 18(3): the Council arbitrates or refers to an institution, and the 1996 Act applies as if there were an arbitration agreement.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Award and challenge.</strong> Award follows; a buyer&rsquo;s challenge under Section 34 requires the seventy-five per cent pre-deposit under Section 19.</div>
</div>

<h2>Silpi Industries: the door is open</h2>
<p>For years, buyers argued — and some High Courts accepted — that the Section 18 mechanism was a one-way street confined to the supplier&rsquo;s claim, leaving the buyer to a separate suit or arbitration. <em>Silpi Industries v. Kerala SRTC</em> settled the point: because Section 18(3) imports the Arbitration and Conciliation Act wholesale, Section 23(2A) of that Act — which expressly permits the respondent to submit a counter-claim or plead set-off within the scope of the arbitration — operates in the statutory arbitration too. The Supreme Court reasoned that splitting claim and counter-claim into separate fora over the same transaction would multiply proceedings and risk conflicting findings.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>Two further holdings of Silpi Industries to keep in view:</strong> the Limitation Act applies to Section 18(3) arbitrations; and the supplier must answer the registration-timing requirements of the Act for its own claim — each side&rsquo;s claim stands on its own statutory feet.</p>
</div>

<h2>Counter-claim, set-off and defence: three different tools</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Defence</th><td>Resists the supplier&rsquo;s claim — defective supply, short delivery, delay, terms not met. Raise from the conciliation stage, with the paper trail: purchase orders, inspection reports, rejection memos, debit notes.</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Set-off</th><td>A money claim pleaded to extinguish or reduce the supplier&rsquo;s claim in the same proceeding, within the arbitration&rsquo;s scope.</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Counter-claim</th><td>An affirmative claim seeking an award in the buyer&rsquo;s favour — damages for defects, delay or breach. Maintainable per Silpi Industries, subject to limitation and to the arbitration&rsquo;s scope.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practical notes for buyers</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Engage at conciliation.</strong> Non-appearance is the classic buyer error: it invites an ex parte award, after which every argument costs a seventy-five per cent deposit. The conciliation stage is free ground to put defences and documents on record.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Plead the counter-claim in the arbitration, with evidence.</strong> A counter-claim is not a recital of grievances; it needs quantification, causation and documents, exactly as in a commercial suit. And check its own limitation first.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Mind Section 16 exposure.</strong> While the proceeding runs, statutory compound interest accrues on any amount ultimately found due. A sound settlement assessment weighs the counter-claim&rsquo;s realistic value against that accrual.</div>
</div>
<p>For suppliers, the mirror lesson: a buyer&rsquo;s counter-claim does not stall the reference, and its merits will be tested in the same award — but prepare for it. The reply to the counter-claim deserves the same rigour as the statement of claim, and the supplier&rsquo;s own record on quality and delivery should be assembled before the reference is filed, not after the counter-claim arrives.</p>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>The MSMED Act tilts the field towards the small supplier deliberately — that is its purpose. <em>Silpi Industries</em> keeps the tilt from becoming a denial of the buyer&rsquo;s forum: everything arising from the transaction can be fought out in one statutory arbitration, with the Act&rsquo;s interest and pre-deposit provisions intact on the supplier&rsquo;s side and ordinary arbitral process available on the buyer&rsquo;s. For Delhi practitioners, where the Delhi MSEFC routes contested references into institutional arbitration, the working consequence is simple: treat the Section 18 arbitration as the single, decisive battlefield for both sides of the account.</p>
